<section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">SELECTED WORK / 03</p><h1>Case studies</h1><p class="section-lede">Three projects in enterprise AI delivery, quality engineering, and employee self-service. Outcomes are described with their evidence limits attached.</p>
<nav class="case-index" aria-label="Case studies"><a href="#case-1">01 · Acquisition command center</a><a href="#case-2">02 · AI-assisted quality engineering</a><a href="#case-3">03 · Enterprise self-service</a></nav></section>
<article class="case-study" id="case-1"><p class="entry-index">CASE STUDY 01 <span>ENTERPRISE AI DELIVERY · CLOUD ARCHITECTURE · ENGINEERING LEADERSHIP</span></p><h2>AI-Powered Acquisition Command Center</h2>
<ul class="case-facts"><li><strong>Delivery</strong> 3 months</li><li><strong>Team</strong> 5 developers, including a technical lead</li><li><strong>Audience</strong> Approximately 50+ users (personal estimate)</li></ul><div class="case-body">
<h3>The challenge</h3><p>An upcoming acquisition required centralized operational visibility during the first 10 days of transition. Playbooks, incidents, requests, and projects were spread across SharePoint, Jira, and ServiceNow. Stakeholders needed to see upcoming activities, delays, and decisions without navigating each system. The solution had to be operational for acquisition Day 1.</p>
<h3>My role and delivery</h3><p>As engineering manager, I led day-to-day delivery and worked with my technical lead on architecture. We were delivering the company’s first Azure-based application. Infrastructure and Information Security provisioned the environment; my team supplied application requirements and configuration direction. I managed priorities, scope decisions, and changing requirements.</p><p>We used Kanban to reassess priorities and negotiate scope. All required functionality was delivered for Day 1.</p>
<h3>The solution</h3><p>A centralized operational dashboard displayed transition activities, with a production AI chatbot for conversational access to operational information. A user could ask, <q>What playbook activities are scheduled in the next four hours, and are any prerequisite activities behind schedule?</q></p><p>The architecture used Azure App Service, Azure Functions, Key Vault, Azure SQL, and Cosmos DB with vector storage for retrieval-augmented generation. The chatbot informed human decisions; it did not create tasks or execute operational changes.</p>
<h3>Incident response</h3><p>On the first morning of hypercare, the dashboard became unavailable because the application scaled down to zero instances. I shifted attention from planned work, investigated, and identified the root cause. The development team corrected the configuration and restored the dashboard before Day 2.</p>
<h3>Outcomes and evidence</h3><ul><li>Delivered the required solution within three months for acquisition Day 1.</li><li>Established the company’s first Azure-based application and put a production AI assistant and cross-platform dashboard into service.</li><li>Supported an estimated audience of more than 50 users.</li><li>Received positive qualitative feedback from CIO, transition, and project-management stakeholders.</li><li>Restored the operational dashboard before Day 2 of hypercare.</li></ul><p class="evidence-note">Audience size is a personal estimate; feedback is qualitative. No productivity, savings, or measured-adoption claim is made.</p>
</div></article>
<article class="case-study" id="case-2"><p class="entry-index">CASE STUDY 02 <span>AI ENABLEMENT · DEVELOPER PRODUCTIVITY · QUALITY ENGINEERING</span></p><h2>From Happy-Path Testing to AI-Assisted Quality Engineering</h2><div class="case-body">
<h3>The challenge</h3><p>Testing often stopped at happy paths, with gaps in negative conditions, boundaries, unusual inputs, and error handling. When I began leading a newly formed AI development team, I wanted stronger quality practices from the outset.</p>
<h3>My approach</h3><p>I developed reusable AI instructions for unit, integration, GUI, release, and performance testing. Stored in GitHub and automatically available in new VS Code sessions, they put guidance into developers’ workflow. Developers could provide Jira ticket information to generate executable unit tests, step-by-step integration and GUI cases, and Gherkin scenarios from acceptance criteria.</p><p>I demonstrated the approach in developer meetings and made its use an expected team practice. Developers were encouraged to improve the shared instructions; contributions to the common GitHub repository became mandatory, with the technical lead reviewing changes against the team’s Agile working agreements.</p>
<h3>Playwright and adoption</h3><p>My team introduced AI-assisted Playwright testing where browser automation had not previously been part of its workflow. On failure, the process examines the failure and selector choices, then re-records the test using existing steps. It retains metadata to inform future re-recordings; developers oversee execution and validation.</p><p>All developers reporting to me use and contribute to the shared instructions. Developers outside my team have experimented with them, but broader adoption is limited and unmeasured. Playwright execution is actively used by my team and is initiated manually rather than through CI/CD.</p>
<h3>Result</h3><p><strong>The supported accomplishment is establishing and adopting an AI-assisted quality engineering capability—not a percentage improvement in defect detection or productivity.</strong></p><p class="evidence-note">Leadership lesson: embed new capabilities in everyday work, set clear team expectations, and distinguish local adoption from organization-wide transformation.</p>
</div></article>
<article class="case-study" id="case-3"><p class="entry-index">CASE STUDY 03 <span>AI ENABLEMENT · AGENTIC ENGINEERING · ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE · PRODUCT DELIVERY</span></p><h2>Building an AI-Enabled Enterprise Self-Service Platform</h2><div class="case-body">
<h3>The challenge</h3><p>Employees needed one simple way to find and manage requests across IT service-management and project-tracking systems. Information was fragmented, making it difficult to see a request’s status, owner, and next step. The goal was access without requiring employees to understand the underlying systems.</p>
<h3>My role</h3><p>As engineering manager, I led four developers and one technical lead. While business requirements were set outside my team, I took a hands-on role in application architecture and user-interface design.</p><p class="pull-quote">“Where is my stuff, and how quickly can I find it?”</p>
<h3>The solution and architecture</h3><p>We delivered a production employee self-service application that opened to a personalized request dashboard. Corporate single sign-on let employees see relevant requests across systems, including status, ownership, updates, and expected completion information. Conversational assistance guides users through established request forms, escalations, project suggestions, and IT questions; it does not autonomously execute those actions.</p><p>I contributed to integration decisions, synchronous versus asynchronous processing, attachment storage, identity-based personalization, and separation of app workflows from underlying enterprise systems. I used AI to support architectural reasoning and implementation.</p>
<h3>A reusable architecture agent</h3><p>I created specialized cloud-architecture and Python-development skills and combined them into an agent. It retrieves enterprise project-management work items, refines requirements, evaluates approaches, and assists with implementation and troubleshooting.</p><p>High-confidence decisions can proceed automatically and are tagged as AI-made decisions. Medium- and low-confidence questions go through human approval. I created the original agent; developers actively use a fork, and I continue to develop a more detailed version for my own architecture work.</p>
<h3>Adoption and outcomes</h3><p>The employee portal reached production and received positive feedback. Employees gained a centralized place to find request information, and the team observed fewer repetitive status inquiries. The architecture agent also became an actively used development capability beyond my individual workflow.</p><p class="evidence-note">These results are qualitative. No productivity percentage, cost savings, or organization-wide adoption claim is made.</p>
</div></article>